smolvm: fsnotify inject: drop a changed file's attributes and pages, for a write-back cache

When the virtiofs server offers a write-back cache, the guest kernel keeps a regular file's size, mtime and pages as
its own and ignores what the server reports, so a change the host makes to the file would stay invisible to the guest
until the inode is dropped. The injected event for a host change now drops the file's attributes and its clean pages,
so the next stat or read goes to the server; pages the guest has dirtied are left alone and the guest's write wins.
Without a write-back cache the server's attributes already carried the change, and this adds nothing the kernel was
not about to do.

Signed-off-by: smolvm <noreply@smolvm>
---
diff --git a/fs/notify/smolvm_inject.c b/fs/notify/smolvm_inject.c
--- a/fs/notify/smolvm_inject.c
+++ b/fs/notify/smolvm_inject.c
@@ -144,8 +144,23 @@
 		take_dentry_name_snapshot(&name, path.dentry);
 	}
 #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_FUSE_FS)
-	if (path.dentry->d_sb->s_magic == FUSE_SUPER_MAGIC)
+	if (path.dentry->d_sb->s_magic == FUSE_SUPER_MAGIC) {
 		fuse_invalidate_entry_cache(path.dentry);
+		/*
+		 * With a write-back cache the kernel trusts its own size, mtime
+		 * and pages for a regular file and ignores what the server
+		 * reports, so a host change would stay invisible: drop the
+		 * attributes and the clean pages here. Dirty pages (the guest is
+		 * writing the same file) are left alone; the guest's write wins.
+		 */
+		if (d_really_is_positive(path.dentry) &&
+		    S_ISREG(d_inode(path.dentry)->i_mode)) {
+			struct inode *inode = d_inode(path.dentry);
+
+			fuse_invalidate_attr(inode);
+			invalidate_inode_pages2(inode->i_mapping);
+		}
+	}
 #endif
 	if (d_really_is_positive(path.dentry))
 		fsnotify_inode(d_inode(path.dentry), mask);
